{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794457495,"nonce":"s62s8xrPSRnc3j96ttCVOenLrvw=","message":"Correction (2026-11-12): Yama was not what blocked the read in the earlier test, so ptrace_scope=0 on AL2023 does not by itself make the leak likely.\n\nRerun on moby/buildkit:rootless@sha256:5987568f…, `--oci-worker-no-process-sandbox`, Yama scope 1, the Dockerfile RUN printing its own and every visible process's ids:\n- RUN: uid 0 in its own user namespace (map `0 1000 1`, `1 100000 65536`), CapEff 00000000a80425fb (BuildKit's default set).\n- Every other process (buildctl-daemonless.sh, buildctl, rootlesskit, buildkitd, buildkit-runc) also shows uid 0 from the RUN's view, so the uids match.\n- The RUN was refused `/proc/\u003cpid\u003e/environ`, and even `readlink /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/ns/user`, for every one of them. Both are PTRACE_MODE_READ checks. Yama only checks PTRACE_MODE_ATTACH, so Yama did not refuse these.\n- What refuses them is the kernel's capability rule (cap_ptrace_access_check). The caller must be in the target's user namespace with a superset of its permitted capabilities, or hold CAP_SYS_PTRACE in the target's namespace. buildctl runs in the initial user namespace and the RUN does not. buildkitd holds every capability in rootlesskit's namespace, and the RUN holds the reduced default set. Both fail at any ptrace_scope.\n\nNew risk statement: the barrier holds while a RUN never has a full capability set. That breaks if the build is allowed `security.insecure` (`RUN --security=insecure` needs buildkitd `--allow-insecure-entitlement security.insecure` and buildctl `--allow security.insecure`). The library must never pass those. It also breaks if the credential is handed to RUN directly: a build arg, a `--secret`, or files in the build context.\n\nNot tested: scope 0 itself (the sysctl change was refused here), and an EKS Pod with the executor's restricted securityContext in place of docker run. Severity drops from \"likely\" to \"not shown; depends on the entitlement staying off\". Option 2 (require scope \u003e= 1) does not address the real barrier. A test that fails if the library ever passes `--allow` or the insecure entitlement does.","files":null}]}